--- Demo 3: render-path ordering test (ADR-043 enable precedes first flush + update) ---

BASELINE (unmodified src/main.rs):
  cargo test --bin tbard test_render_path_enable_write_precedes_first_flush_and_update
  → test result: ok. 1 passed (calls[0] == "enable"; enable_idx < update_idx;
    write_log non-empty + first_write > 0)

ADVERSARIAL: python script
  (a) removed the scanout-begin `if let Err(e) = backlight.enable() { ... }` block
      (replaced with a comment);
  (b) inserted `let _ = backlight.enable();` AFTER the loop-bottom
      `backlight.update();` (so enable runs after the first flush + update,
      then the OneShotShutdownDisplay breaks the loop at the next top check).
  Added an eprintln of the captured call order right before the first assert.

TEST OUTPUT against the planted tree:
  ADVERSARIAL CALL ORDER: ["update", "enable"]
  thread 'tests::test_render_path_enable_write_precedes_first_flush_and_update'
  panicked at src/main.rs:1450:9:
  assertion `left == right` failed: ADR 043: backlight.enable() must be the
  FIRST backlight call on the render path (scanout-begin), before the first
  flush and the loop-bottom update; got order ["update", "enable"]
    left: "update"
   right: "enable"
  test result: FAILED. 0 passed; 1 failed

RESTORE + re-verify:
  test result: ok. 1 passed

VERDICT: the render-path ordering test is NON-VACUOUS — moving `enable()`
after the loop-bottom `update()` (i.e. after the first flush) flips the
recorded call order to ["update", "enable"] and the test FAILS at the
`calls[0] == "enable"` assertion. The ordering is ASSERTED, not implied.

--- MockBacklight write_log non-vacuity ---

Inspection (src/providers/mock_backlight.rs):
  enable()  → self.write_log.push_back(v) at line 53 (v = brightness.clamp(max))
  set_brightness() → self.write_log.push_back(v) at line 38
The render-path test asserts `!backlight.inner.write_log().is_empty()` AND
`*front() > 0` after real_main runs one iteration. The adversarial demo
above (moving enable after update) still records BOTH writes (update's
internal set_brightness path is a no-op on MockBacklight because the trait
`update()` default is a no-op — wait: MockBacklight does NOT override
update(), so update() records NOTHING; only enable() records). Confirmed:
in the adversarial run the write_log would still be non-empty (enable
recorded), but calls[0] == "update" fails the ordering assertion first.
The write_log assertion is a SEPARATE, non-vacuous check that the
enable-write produced a recorded non-zero value.
